  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA well established mid/upper Omega Block will continue through\nearly next week, characterized by a couple of closed lows\nrotating into the Four Corners region and Rockies and another\ntrough and associated closed low gradually lifting northeastward\nfrom the Mid Atlantic region, with a sharp ridge in between over\nthe central CONUS. The southern Great Lakes and Ohio Valley\nregions will remain favorably placed beneath upper level\nconfluence, maintaining strong surface high pressure of Canadian\norigin. This surface high is centered over eastern Ontario and\nQuebec this afternoon and will gradually progress south into the\nsouthern Appalachians and Mid Atlantic region by late Sunday \nand Monday. This will continue the familiar pattern of mostly \nclear skies, light winds, and seasonable temperatures through \nMonday, but afternoon highs will be slightly warmer as a SE \nreturn flow develops (mid/upper 60s Saturday and upper 60s/low \n70s Sunday and Monday). The main weather impact will be \novernight and morning fog. Light NE winds off Lake Erie again \nthis afternoon have advected slightly higher dew points into NW \nand north central Ohio, and this low-level moisture will aid in \nsmall temp/dew point depressions and low-level saturation \nbeneath the strong inversion again tonight. The fog was locally \ndense this morning across NW Ohio and lingered through about \n17Z, so expect a similar scenario again late tonight through \nSaturday morning. The most widespread fog will occur in NW and \nnorth central Ohio, but patchy fog is possible elsewhere as \nwell. This same scenario will likely play out again Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning, but more of a SE flow during the day \nSaturday should keep low- level moisture in-check, so do not \nthink it will be as widespread.\n\nA weak cold front associated with a mid/upper shortwave dropping\nthrough the eastern Great Lakes will drop through the area\nMonday, but very dry air should lead to a dry frontal passage.\nCooler conditions are expected post front Tuesday as another\nCanadian high builds down into the Great Lakes, but the pattern\nlooks to evolve into more of a quasi-zonal mid/upper flow \nacross the CONUS as the week progresses due to the low/trough \noff the East Coast pulling away and strong Pacific jet extension\ninto the West Coast. This will support a warming trend for mid \nand late week.",
